{"name":"Buckwheat Mauve","hex":"#b9a4b0","fetched":"2026-10-11","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"buckwheat","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["An Asian plant, of the species Fagopyrum esculentum. .mw-parser-output .defdate{font-size:smaller}","The fruit of this plant used as a pseudocereal."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/buckwheat"},{"term":"mauve","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A rich purple synthetic dye, which faded easily, briefly popular c. 1859‒1873 and now called mauveine.","A pale purple or violet colour, like the colour of the dye after it has faded."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Having a pale purple colour."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/mauve"}],"entries":[{"title":"Buckwheat","blocks":[{"text":"Buckwheat or common buckwheat is a flowering plant in the knotweed family Polygonaceae cultivated for its grain-like seeds and as a cover crop. Buckwheat cultivation originated around the 6th millennium BC in the region of what is now Yunnan Province in southwestern China. The name \"buckwheat\" is used for several other species, such as Fagopyrum tataricum, a domesticated food plant raised in Asia."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a7/Japanese_Buckwheat_Flower.JPG/330px-Japanese_Buckwheat_Flower.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Buckwheat","wikidata":"Q132734"},{"title":"Mauve","blocks":[{"text":"Mauve is a pale purple color named after the mallow flower. The first use of the word mauve as a color was in 1796–1798 according to the Oxford English Dictionary, but its use seems to have been rare before 1859. Another name for the color is mallow, with the first recorded use of mallow as a color name in English in 1611."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/32/Wilde_Malve.JPG/330px-Wilde_Malve.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumb